states.expected_output:
The expected outputs of the processing will be:  

-​​ A report of findings to academic, clinical, and policy stakeholders, including aggregate analyses of cancer incidence, stage at diagnosis, diagnostic pathways, and mortality outcomes across the study period. 

​​- Submissions to peer-reviewed journals, including final publications reporting aggregated findings on diagnostic delay, cancer progression, and screening strategies across the duration of the project.​ 

​- Presentations to stakeholders and engagement activities, including a final stakeholder workshop to support interpretation of findings and their implications for cancer policy, screening, and service planning. 

- ​Presentations at appropriate academic and policy conferences, as part of dissemination of findings relating to cancer early detection, diagnostic pathways, and screening strategies. 

- ​Development of analytical models and simulation frameworks, including multistate models of cancer progression and microsimulation tools to evaluate diagnostic timelines and screening strategies. 

Within 12 months of data access:

For Workstream 1:
•	Aggregate statistical summaries of age-, ethnicity-, and cancer-specific incidence and mortality patterns across the 12 included cancers;
•	Aggregate analyses of stage at diagnosis, routes to diagnosis, and related mortality outcomes across cancer types;

For Workstream 2:
•	Development of ethnicity-specific population-level natural history models for breast cancer;
•	Aggregate analyses of breast cancer mode of detection, including screen-detected and clinically detected cancers;
•	Development of the microsimulation framework required to evaluate the population-level effects of alternative breast cancer screening strategies;
•	Early PPIE-informed discussion of emerging findings, particularly where results relate to fairness, acceptability, and ethnic differences in early detection;

Within 24 months of data access:

For Workstream 1:
•	Development of modelling work on diagnostic pathways and the impact of diagnostic delay across the 12 included cancers;
•	Aggregate analyses of the potential economic burden associated with alternative diagnostic delay scenarios and cancer-specific diagnostic timelines;

For Workstream 2:
•	Preparation and submission of peer-reviewed manuscripts arising from the breast cancer natural history;
•	Conference presentations and academic dissemination of findings relevant to breast cancer screening policy, including how these alternative screening strategies may affect cancer detection, mortality outcomes, the extent to which alternative strategies may reduce inequalities across ethnic groups, and the potential economic and service implications at the population level.

Within 36 months of data access:

For Workstream 1:
•	Peer-reviewed publications reporting final aggregated findings from the diagnostic pathway and diagnostic delay work across the 12 included cancers;
•	Policy-facing outputs synthesising implications for cancer-specific diagnostic timelines, service planning, and inequalities in early diagnosis across population groups;
•	Final stakeholder dissemination activities, including discussion of implications for early detection policy and cancer diagnostic pathways where appropriate.

​​​​​The outputs will not contain NHS England Data and will only contain aggregated information with small numbers suppressed as appropriate in line with the relevant disclosure rules for the dataset(s) from which the information was derived.​ ​

​​The outputs will be communicated to relevant recipients through the following dissemination channels:  
•	Journals – submission of peer‑ reviewed publications reporting aggregated findings from the analysis of diagnostic delay, cancer progression, and screening strategies. 
•	​Workshops involving academic, clinical, and policy stakeholders – including a planned stakeholder workshop to support interpretation of findings and discussion of implications for cancer policy, screening, and diagnostic. 
•	​Briefing documents provided to relevant stakeholders – including policy-facing outputs synthesising implications for service planning, diagnostic timelines, and inequalities in early diagnosis. 
•	​Presentations at appropriate conferences – dissemination of findings through academic and professional forums relating to cancer research, early detection, and public health 
•	​Public reports – production of aggregated, anonymised statistical outputs suitable for wider dissemination, with no release of record-level data.  
•	​Direct engagement with stakeholders and communities – including ongoing involvement of patient and public contributors (PPIE) in interpreting findings and supporting clear communication of results. 
•	​Public promotion of the research – through appropriate academic and stakeholder engagement activities, ensuring findings are communicated in a way that supports understanding and avoids misinterpretation.
